    "title": "Governor of a State: Appointment, Discretionary Powers, Ordinance Making (Art 213) & Constitutional Role",
    "subtitle": "Articles 153 to 162, Dual Role of Governor, Discretionary Powers (Art 163), Article 213 Ordinance & Article 200 Assent",
    "overview": "Analyze the constitutional duality, discretionary powers, ordinance prerogatives, and gubernatorial assent of the Governor.",
    "summary": "The Governor is the <strong>constitutional head of the state</strong> and a vital constitutional link between the Centre and the State. Appointed by the President under <strong>Article 155</strong>, the Governor holds office during the pleasure of the President (Article 156). Under <strong>Article 154</strong>, executive power of the state is vested in the Governor, exercised on the aid and advice of the State Council of Ministers headed by the Chief Minister (Article 163), except in matters where the Governor is required to act in his personal <strong>discretion</strong>.",
    "fullNotes": "<h3>1. Constitutional Office & Appointment (Articles 153 to 157)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Article 153:</strong> There shall be a Governor for each State. The <strong>7th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1956</strong> added a proviso facilitating the appointment of the <strong>same person as Governor for two or more States</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Appointment & Pleasure Doctrine (Articles 155 & 156):</strong> Appointed by the <strong>President under his hand and seal</strong>. Holds office during the <strong>pleasure of the President</strong> for a normal term of 5 years. Has no security of tenure; may be transferred or removed by the President at any time without reason stated (though <em>B.P. Singhal v. Union of India, 2010</em> held that removal cannot be arbitrary, mala fide, or based on political changes at the Centre).</li>\n  <li><strong>Qualifications (Article 157):</strong> Must be a citizen of India and have completed the age of <strong>35 years</strong>. Conventions: (1) Should be an outsider not belonging to the state, and (2) Chief Minister of the state should be consulted before appointment (Sarkaria Commission recommendations).</li>\n  <li><strong>Sarojini Naidu (Historic Milestone):</strong> Sarojini Naidu was appointed the <strong>first woman Governor of an Indian state</strong>, serving as Governor of the United Provinces (Uttar Pradesh) from August 15, 1947 to March 2, 1949.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Constitutional Powers & Discretion (Articles 161, 163, 200, 213)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Discretionary Powers (Article 163):</strong> Unlike the President (who has almost no explicit constitutional discretion), Article 163(1) expressly recognizes that the Governor may act <strong>in his discretion</strong>. Article 163(2) provides that the Governor's decision on whether a matter falls in his discretion is final and cannot be questioned in court. Key discretionary areas:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Reservation of a Bill for the consideration of the President (Article 200).</li>\n      <li>Recommendation of President's Rule in the state under Article 356.</li>\n      <li>Selection of Chief Minister when no party has a clear majority in the Assembly.</li>\n      <li>Dismissal of Council of Ministers when it loses the confidence of the Assembly.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Assent to Bills & Reservation for President (Article 200 & 201):</strong> When a Bill passed by the State Legislature is presented to the Governor, he has 4 options: (1) Assent, (2) Withhold assent, (3) Return Bill for reconsideration (if not a Money Bill), or (4) <strong>Reserve the Bill for the consideration of the President</strong>. The Governor MUST reserve any Bill that derogates from the powers of the High Court so as to endanger its constitutional position.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ordinance Making Power (Article 213):</strong> Can promulgate ordinances when the State Legislative Assembly (or either House in bicameral states) is in recess. Ceases to operate <strong>six weeks from the reassembly</strong> of the state legislature. The Governor cannot make certain ordinances without previous instruction from the President (e.g., if a Bill containing the same provisions required presidential sanction).</li>\n  <li><strong>Pardoning Powers (Article 161):</strong> The Governor can grant pardons, reprieves, respites, or remissions of punishment for offences against laws relating to matters to which the state executive power extends. <em>Crucial Distinction from President:</em> The Governor <strong>CANNOT pardon a sentence of death</strong> (only President under Art 72 can pardon death), though the Governor can suspend, remit, or commute a death sentence. Governor also cannot pardon court-martial sentences.</li>\n</ul>",
