    "overview": "Master the bicameral structure, seat allocations, election modes, and legislative procedures of the Uttar Pradesh Legislature.",
    "summary": "Under <strong>Article 168</strong>, the Legislature of Uttar Pradesh is <strong>Bicameral</strong>, consisting of the Governor and two Houses: the <strong>Legislative Assembly (Vidhan Sabha)</strong> and the <strong>Legislative Council (Vidhan Parishad)</strong>. Uttar Pradesh is one of only 6 Indian states that have a bicameral legislature (along with Bihar, Maharashtra, Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, and Telangana). With <strong>403 elected MLAs</strong> and <strong>100 MLCs</strong>, UP possesses the largest state legislature in India.",
    "fullNotes": "<h3>1. The Legislative Assembly (Vidhan Sabha – Article 170)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Composition & Strength:</strong> Article 170 fixes the strength of a State Legislative Assembly at a maximum of 500 and a minimum of 60 members.\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Uttar Pradesh Vidhan Sabha:</em> <strong>403 directly elected members</strong> (largest in India). (The 104th Amendment Act, 2019 discontinued the nomination of 1 Anglo-Indian member under Art 333).</li>\n      <li><em>Reservations:</em> <strong>84 seats reserved for Scheduled Castes (SC)</strong> and <strong>2 seats reserved for Scheduled Tribes (ST)</strong> (Sonbhadra: Obra and Duddhi seats).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Qualifications & Tenure:</strong> Minimum age is <strong>25 years</strong>. Normal term is 5 years, subject to dissolution by the Governor. Speaker and Deputy Speaker elected from among MLAs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. The Legislative Council (Vidhan Parishad – Article 171)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Composition & Strength:</strong> Total strength of a Legislative Council cannot exceed <strong>one-third of the total strength of the Legislative Assembly</strong>, and cannot be less than 40.\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Uttar Pradesh Vidhan Parishad:</em> <strong>100 members</strong> (the largest Legislative Council in India).</li>\n      <li><em>Election Breakdown (Article 171(3)):</em>\n        <ol>\n          <li><strong>1/3rd (38 members in UP):</strong> Elected by members of local authorities (Municipalities, Zilla Panchayats, Block Pramukhs).</li>\n          <li><strong>1/3rd (36 members in UP):</strong> Elected by MLAs from among persons who are not MLAs.</li>\n          <li><strong>1/12th (8 members in UP):</strong> Elected by Graduates of at least 3 years' standing residing in the state (Graduates' Constituencies).</li>\n          <li><strong>1/12th (8 members in UP):</strong> Elected by Teachers of at least 3 years' standing in secondary schools or higher (Teachers' Constituencies).</li>\n          <li><strong>1/6th (10 members in UP):</strong> <strong>Nominated by the Governor</strong> from persons having special knowledge in Literature, Science, Art, Cooperative Movement, and Social Service. (Note: 'Cooperative Movement' is present in Governor's nomination list under Art 171, but absent in President's RS nomination list under Art 80).</li>\n        </ol>\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Nature & Tenure:</strong> Permanent body, <strong>not subject to dissolution</strong>. Members serve a <strong>6-year term</strong>, with <strong>one-third retiring every second year</strong>. Minimum age is <strong>30 years</strong>. Presided over by a Chairman elected by MLCs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Creation & Abolition (Article 169):</strong> Parliament can abolish an existing Legislative Council or create a new one if the Legislative Assembly of the state passes a resolution to that effect by a <strong>Special Majority</strong> (majority of total membership + 2/3rd members present and voting). The law is passed by Parliament by a Simple Majority (not an amendment under Art 368).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Legislative Relationship: Assembly Supremacy</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Money Bills:</strong> Can originate ONLY in Vidhan Sabha. Vidhan Parishad has only <strong>14 days</strong> to return it with recommendations.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ordinary Bills:</strong> Vidhan Parishad has <strong>NO veto power</strong>. It can delay an Ordinary Bill passed by the Assembly for a maximum of <strong>3 months in the first instance</strong>. If passed again by the Assembly, the Parishad can delay it for <strong>1 more month</strong>. Thus, the maximum delay the Vidhan Parishad can cause to an Ordinary Bill is <strong>4 months</strong>, after which the Bill is deemed passed. There is <strong>NO provision for a Joint Sitting</strong> in the State Legislature!</li>\n</ul>",
